  "Question": "If \\(y=\\sqrt{x^2-2x+1}\\) \\({}+\\sqrt{x^2+2x+1},\\) then \\(y\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2x\\)",
    "B": "\\(2(x+1)\\)",
    "C": "\\(0\\)",
    "D": "\\(|x-1|+|x+1|\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Both expressions under the radicals are perfect squares:\n\\[\nx^2-2x+1=(x-1)^2,\\qquad x^2+2x+1=(x+1)^2.\n\\]\nThey are therefore nonnegative for every real \\(x\\). For any real number \\(u\\), the principal square root satisfies \\(\\sqrt{u^2}=|u|\\), since the square root must be nonnegative. Applying this to each term gives\n\\[\ny=\\sqrt{(x-1)^2}+\\sqrt{(x+1)^2}\n=|x-1|+|x+1|.\n\\]\nThis identity includes \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=-1\\), where one radical is zero. The answer is choice D.",
